{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness","id":1195123,"title":"World of Darkness (Hold the Darkness)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-13T21:45:16-07:00","id":1195123,"title":"World of Darkness (Hold the Darkness)","content":"Am I stupid for thinking that it's possible to play the World of Darkness as less, well... dark?<br /> <br /> Maybe it's just that I'm an ultra-idealistic millennial manchild, but I've always seen the WoD as a great urban fantasy setting that can run the whole spectrum of tones and moods without sacrificing its famous thematic complexity. I enjoy it as a world with dark shadows, yes, but brilliant lights and amazing things as well. I just don't see the fun in making the answer to everything &quot;It's the World of Darkness, so everything's terrible.&quot;<br /> <br /> Am I alone in this? Am I profoundly missing the point, or does my interpretation hold some merit?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T03:10:51-07:00","id":1195199,"title":"","content":"Not at all. 2nd Ed started really pushing the everyone is dark when you look close enough angle, but backed off later into Revised because you need contrast or it gets boring.<br /> <br /> 20th has sort of backpeddled a bit, but there is nothing wrong with wanting a brighter WoD, and plenty of good stories can be had. Granted, it might not be for everyone, and a lot of people enjoy WoD games specifically because it is darker than most other settings, so do keep that in mind.<br /> <br /> Just change the theme to urban fantasy, make a few setting tweeks, and go. One game I have been tossing around running for a while is a city where Vampires, Mages, Werewolves, Wraiths, etc... have come together as a sort of city council that deals with a variety of supernatural (and mundane) threats, not to mention interpersonal conflicts, with the intention of one day revealing themselves to the world without being hunted or ruining the domain.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are still Garou, with Tribes, spirits, etc.., Vampires are Kindred with ancient Noddist lore, etc... <br /> They just all have been told to play nice, police the superatural up, and don't cause trouble as they want to try to make a better world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://t0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcTAO8runv-CR5dodCXgw_8wdJype5yunYCODWdZiBAwUP5WFVv8\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://ksr-ugc.imgix.net/assets/003/593/392/92a29c2836b988505781770a32720148_original.png?v=1428699067&amp;w=700&amp;h=&amp;fit=max&amp;auto=format&amp;lossless=true&amp;s=69ffdde357a0cdd88ee89934622443f9\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img692/7017/hh2support.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img703/6636/6kvo.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1721-beckett","name":"Beckett","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1721_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1077"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T03:18:06-07:00","id":1195201,"title":"","content":"I mostly run Mage and while there are dark things happening, the overall theme is about overcoming those things and creating a better world, even if only on the scale of a neighborhood. Half the fun is the wonders (and not the item kind) they encounter.<br /> <br /> This is particularly true if you run with the more nuanced Technocrats of the Revised Convention Books who aren\u2019t monolithic crushers of the human spirit, but fellow mages who made a different call than the Traditions on where they fall on the whole Liberty vs. Security spectrum and that some members of both sides of the old Ascension War are crappy human beings, a few are bonifide heroes and most are somewhere in between.<br /> <br /> Also interesting in this regard is the notion in Mage20 that the whole \u201cend of the world\u201d and its related garbage came and went. The world is still here spinning away and reports that magic was on the verge of death turned out to be greatly exaggerated. That\u2019s a massive \u201chope spot\u201d all on its own; kinda like going from the darkest days of the Battle of Britain in 1940 or in the days after Pearl Harbor when the Japanese swept in and pushed back American forces again and again and all hope seemed lost to life in 1946 when the Axis powers had been soundly crushed and the Cold War not quite begun.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I totally agree you can run the World of Darkness setting without making the darkness all consuming and Mage is one of the better windows on that version of the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T10:44:47-07:00","id":1195370,"title":"","content":"You could do mage and changelings as Urban Fantasy with less grim dark rather well. Even Mummy the Reborn is about optimism in the face of doom.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-14T17:32:01-07:00","id":1195542,"title":"","content":"I've found it's fairly easy to run stuff in the vein of, say, the first season or two of Angel or The Dresden Files books. Usually using caitiff, sorcerers (sometimes orphans), mediums, old style mummies, psychics, changelings and sometimes risen or tribeless werewolves or fera. .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-19T06:53:55-07:00","id":1197168,"title":"","content":"I hate to say it, but I'm actually starting to slide even further: most, if not all, of the World of Darkness is fundamentally, immutably silly on a structural level. The fact that it tends to take itself very seriously only exacerbates this tendency.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-21T12:27:43-07:00","id":1198071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Beckett</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1195199#post1195199\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not at all. 2nd Ed started really pushing the everyone is dark when you look close enough angle, but backed off later into Revised because you need contrast or it gets boring.<br /> <br /> 20th has sort of backpeddled a bit, but there is nothing wrong with wanting a brighter WoD, and plenty of good stories can be had. Granted, it might not be for everyone, and a lot of people enjoy WoD games specifically because it is darker than most other settings, so do keep that in mind.<br /> <br /> Just change the theme to urban fantasy, make a few setting tweeks, and go. One game I have been tossing around running for a while is a city where Vampires, Mages, Werewolves, Wraiths, etc... have come together as a sort of city council that deals with a variety of supernatural (and mundane) threats, not to mention interpersonal conflicts, with the intention of one day revealing themselves to the world without being hunted or ruining the domain.<br /> <br /> Werewolves are still Garou, with Tribes, spirits, etc.., Vampires are Kindred with ancient Noddist lore, etc...<br /> They just all have been told to play nice, police the superatural up, and don't cause trouble as they want to try to make a better world.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The current chronicle I'm running is set in a fictional city run by a Camarilla Prince. However, the threat of Baali teaming up with Black Spiral Dancers and Fallen Raveners has caused the players to all come together. One of which is a Mummy (that doesn't even know that he's a mummy or what a mummy is, the vampires assume he's some kind of a mage) another is a Fallen recently free from the Abyss, two are vampires of the local court and the last player is a setite from outside the city interested in all the chaos that has started to pop up in the domain. I still have a lot of darker themes and occurences. But, there's also some fun urban fantasy stuff going on. Like the Mummy guy built up a reputation as a local manwhore and &quot;lord of the dance&quot; by putting on an epic performance at a strip/sex club that's an elysium run by one of the Torreador. Point is, you can definatley be very dark and still have moments of levity. Just thought I'd share cause I liked your crossover 'council' idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23951-acwrig2","name":"acwrig2","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"112"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T10:58:54-07:00","id":1200135,"title":"","content":"\u200b\u200bI don't think you're stupid or alone or wrong at all for wanting a less &quot;grimdark&quot; WoD. You really don't have to remove a lot, depending on the setting, to get a little blue sky and some silver linings into your world. My experience is with WTA, so I'll speak mainly from that perspective. If you don't want the apocalypse to be inevitably coming within this generation, just don't make it so. You know how often people in the real world are always trying to predict the apocalypse, and how people in the real world are always seeing &quot;signs&quot; of the apocalypse, and how vague apocalyptic prophecy tends to be? Turn that into the reality the Garou are facing: sure, there are signs of the apocalypse everywhere, but there have always been signs of the apocalypse here and there, so it's futile to try to forecast it, and with enough wisdom, determination, and luck, the Gaian spirits and fera might have hope start turning the tide, beat back the Wyrm's corruption and the Weaver's webs gradually over time, and maybe put off the apocalypse indefinitely. It's the old &quot;we're not dead yet&quot; and &quot;never give up&quot; attitude they already have, with the &quot;hope that springs eternal in the human breast&quot; added to the mix because &quot;it ain't over until it's over.&quot; For the vampires, maybe all you have to do is offer them a real, achievable hope of finding a way to keep the Beast at bay indefinitely, give the religious ones a reason to hope their souls aren't hopelessly damned, and then the only thing they ultimately can't avoid is the threat of ennui.<br /> <br /> In my humble opinion, all you have to do to rescue the WoD from the grimdark mood is to remove the inevitability of the overwhelming doom that threatens the various kinds of monsters, and replace it with a very serious threat of doom, but one that they have some measure of hope to overcome. Really that's what I see the introductory comic in the W20 core book trying to do. Once you've got some real, serious hope for the characters to reach towards, you can add more subtle things, like make Pentex less powerful, make the werewolves more willing to make truces with the vampires, etc. It really can be, as others have said, a good urban fantasy setting, approaching something like the &quot;Being Human&quot; TV show, where survival, redemption, and virtue are actually possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T11:25:59-07:00","id":1200495,"title":"","content":"In hindsight, I'd have to say that, over the years, my Vampire games have tended to be more dark in tone and mood than the others. Werewolf has tended to be brutal and often violent, but with the idea that the PCs are taking action that isn't pointless. My Mage games have tended to be dark-ish with the general mood of the PCs being a potential light in the darkness and making a difference (even if sometimes only on a small or local level). The handful of times I've run Changeling, it's tended to be similar to my Mage games, just slightly less dark (likely because of the lack of ever-present totalitarianism shading everything) and occasionally more whimsical. Weirdly, the Wraith games I've run have tended to be the most hopeful, but that may just be because I've always approached the game as one about second chances.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T15:41:43-07:00","id":1200614,"title":"","content":"No, it's ok! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I've always seen the WoD more as a dark urban fantasy setting (with the different games harboring different levels of incorporating personal horror elements), with a heavily stylized dark gothic-punk aesthetics (which I quite like) than a strict horror slash social critique one.<br /> <br /> Yes it is those too and don't get me wrong, it's reflection of real-world phenomena and in-your-face with the dark attitude are things I always loved, but truly, I always found the overly grimdark you-cant-play-anything-than-lowly-nobodies-and-can't-change-anything-and-the-whole-world-is-shit attitude some people prefer boring. Also, how long we could mourn the terrible loss of humanity and the terror of having to drink blood, truly?<br /> <br /> No, WoD is a power fnatasy too and if you have power, you could change things and if there's the possibility of change, there's hope.<br /> <br /> Also, WoD has a certain coolness factor and I like to play into it from time-to-time. Just read the fiction.<br /> <br /> So yes, I think it's totally possible to play it as less dark. Nothing's wrong with that.<br /> <br /> Fundamentalyy, I think the difference between an average lighter urban fantasy and WoD boils down to this: in an average UF, most people are good, even if they're mediocre, or fallible, but fundamentally good. Even supernaturals keep things like community, love and such on high regard (in a sense, they are more human than WoD's supnat residents) and they are willing to play nice, even in the face of long-standing prejudices. There is darkness, but that is something that comes from the outside, from demons, crazy vampires, cultists, dark mages, etc. or from the abnormal or broken individuals. In a sense, it's a fundamentally Western, dare I say, American mindset.<br /> <br /> In WoD, however, the power hungry, selfish, bordering on-or full-blown sociopathic and much more inhumane are arguably the norm among the supernatural, while the mundanes are mindless sheeps most of the time, apathetic wageslaves. People, in general are just bad in the WoD, either because humans are just less good than they think they are, or because of the influence of the supernatural. It's a much more cynical mindset and not everyone is comfortable with it.<br /> <br /> So, in a nutshell, in an average UF, the world is in the light and you defending it from the darkness. It's a superheroes' world. In the WoD, the world is dark, or at least heavily dimmed dark-gray and you try to defend and spread the rare sparks of light. Or just want to rule the darkness as one of the monsters. It's a villains' world.<br /> <br /> Of course, this is a scale and you could do your game at any point you want in-between. It's not even consistent between the games. with Vampire, I tend to lean more toward dark, with Mage, to a more hopeful style.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 03-28-2018, 07:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-30T22:21:30-07:00","id":1201538,"title":"","content":"Dude, I've been running my Vampire games as a &quot;less dark&quot; World of Darkness for years.<br /> <br /> No Metaplot, No Personal Horror, No Gothic-Punk stuff (my games are more anime-influenced), Vampires can be heroic good guys if they choose to do so, more Trenchcoats and Katanas than you can shake a stick at, and the 90's fan-game Senshi: The Merchandising is declared canon in my personal games, while Kindred of the East and Demon: The Fallen are declared non-canon.<br /> <br /> I personally found the idea of Personal Horror to be stuffy and boring, but other people like it and there's nothing wrong with that either. You do you, and I'll do me and so on.<br /> <br /> Long story short, it's YOUR game. Make it what you want it to be. As long as everyone is having fun, there is NO &quot;wrong way&quot; to play World of Darkness or any other role-playing game for that matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-31T11:42:27-07:00","id":1201676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201538#post1201538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dude, I've been running my Vampire games as a &quot;less dark&quot; World of Darkness for years.<br /> <br /> No Metaplot, No Personal Horror, No Gothic-Punk stuff (my games are more anime-influenced), Vampires can be heroic good guys if they choose to do so, more Trenchcoats and Katanas than you can shake a stick at, and the 90's fan-game Senshi: The Merchandising is declared canon in my personal games, while Kindred of the East and Demon: The Fallen are declared non-canon.<br /> <br /> I personally found the idea of Personal Horror to be stuffy and boring, but other people like it and there's nothing wrong with that either. You do you, and I'll do me and so on.<br /> <br /> Long story short, it's YOUR game. Make it what you want it to be. As long as everyone is having fun, there is NO &quot;wrong way&quot; to play World of Darkness or any other role-playing game for that matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not quite with you on the starting statements, there. Anime as a medium has angst o'plenty, and even action series often end up in maudlin soul-searching. Also, while Vampire's metaplot, as always, is a hot mess, the metaplot of Werewolf was there to give examples of large-scale operations that characters could spearhead. While other games could occasionally have some form of nebulous existential crisis, it wasn't made into a meter, either, so personal horror really just remains the focus of Vampire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-31T15:14:48-07:00","id":1201714,"title":"","content":"Most of the drama and action oriented anime I've seen, especially the urban fantasy/superpowers and sci-fi stuff, is pretty much dead on &quot;Gothic-Punk&quot;, at least according to the definition given in an article/essay from White Wolf Magazine way back in the day. Essentially the weight of the past and of the vast impersonal powers of the present bearing down oppressively on the world and those in it, contrasted with an attitude of rebellion against the status quo of banality, surrender and ignorance. Often the dignity and dreams of the individual striving against uncaring or corrupt forces who want to crush such things. It's not dark clothes and odd haircuts; it's the ideas behind those things in the first place.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-31T16:59:08-07:00","id":1201743,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201714#post1201714\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Most of the drama and action oriented anime I've seen, especially the urban fantasy/superpowers and sci-fi stuff, is pretty much dead on &quot;Gothic-Punk&quot;, at least according to the definition given in an article/essay from White Wolf Magazine way back in the day. Essentially the weight of the past and of the vast impersonal powers of the present bearing down oppressively on the world and those in it, contrasted with an attitude of rebellion against the status quo of banality, surrender and ignorance. Often the dignity and dreams of the individual striving against uncaring or corrupt forces who want to crush such things. It's not dark clothes and odd haircuts; it's the ideas behind those things in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>And that is precisely why I hate, <i>hate</i> it, when people are sayin &quot;oh, gothic-punk is dead, it's so '90s, etc.&quot; Yes, including the current WW folks. What they want to do is pretty much gothic-punk, philosophically, as gothic-punk was described in the books.<br /> <br /> What those people usually mean is, they feel the goth/gothic and punk aesthetics the books and most especially Vampire went for are outmoded and that kids these days don't wear the same clothes and listen to the same music. Or, more precisely, that those subcultures aren't as big a manistream rage as they were back then. That's fine enough, I guess, however I still like the style and to me, it always will be Vtm's style and I'll miss it. And I wasn't even a teenager in the '90s, or a real part of any subcultural scene.<br /> <br /> Regardless, gothic-punk is an <i>attitude</i>, first and foremost and intrinsically baked into the setting as long as there are age-old conspiracies and monsters and secrets and as long as there are Anarchs, opposing the old, opressive regime and things like that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T03:41:45-07:00","id":1201887,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201676#post1201676\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not quite with you on the starting statements, there. Anime as a medium has angst o'plenty, and even action series often end up in maudlin soul-searching. Also, while Vampire's metaplot, as always, is a hot mess, the metaplot of Werewolf was there to give examples of large-scale operations that characters could spearhead. While other games could occasionally have some form of nebulous existential crisis, it wasn't made into a meter, either, so personal horror really just remains the focus of Vampire.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know there's a lot of angst in anime, trust me. But to me, the concept of personal horror is a specific model of angst that I do not like. The metaplot was never to my liking, and as for the whole Gothic-Punk thing, it's just an aesthetic that I don't care for, tied to two subcultures that aren't really my thing. That is all. I meant no offense to anyone.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T11:32:13-07:00","id":1201973,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201887#post1201887\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But to me, the concept of personal horror is a specific model of angst that I do not like.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, could you please describe, as accuratelly, as you can, that specific kind of angst, as you're perceiving it? Also, that it is in the books, or something you perceived in the fanbase's attitude?<br /> <br /> Just to be clear, I'm not nitpicking, I'm really curious! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The metaplot was never to my liking, </div> </div> </div>Understandable, it's not a thing for a lot of people. I like it and in general, metaplot in rpgs aren't a bad thing for me, but hey.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and as for the whole Gothic-Punk thing, it's just an aesthetic that I don't care for, tied to two subcultures that aren't really my thing. That is all. I meant no offense to anyone. </div> </div> </div>Please, don't get it as me trying to argue about your taste, or choices, those are totally fine, I just want to understand it. It's just a bit strange to me, for two resons:<br /> <br /> 1. The books discussed at length, that gothic-punk is an attitude in WoD, baked into the themes and premises of the settings, not primarily just an aesthetical choice. Sure, it got tangled up with the goth/punk visual style of the game and it's not surprising that a lot of people are confusing the two, but still, the books made it clear what they mean by gothic-punk, philosophycally. I'm just curious, how or why you got to see gothic-punk as just aesthetics, despite the books' descriptions?<br /> <br /> 2. About the aesthetical side: I remember you said that you had bad experiences with said subcultures, so I understand that you don't care for that. However, how you got to like the game, especially 1e, if you don't like the aesthetics even a bit? I'm asking, because for me, the visuals of VtM wasl always an ever-present and very important part of the full impression the game made, so, I'd automatically assume that if someone likes any iteration of the game, they possibly like the aesthetics of the books, at some level. Or do you use text-only versions?<br /> <br /> <br /> Ultimately, if you don't like the themes of gothic-punk and don't like the aesthetics of the game, what drove you to it, or more accurately, why did you pick it up the first place?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T14:28:31-07:00","id":1202020,"title":"","content":"<b>Gothic</b> (<i>not</i> Goth) refers to the Gothic literary genre of the 18th and 19th centuries and it's modern decedents, as well as it's spin-offs (Southern Gothic, German &quot;Shudder Novels&quot;, Pulp Horror, Hammer Horror films, etc.) and to certain traits of the late medieval architecture that played such a huge part in it. Said architecture tends to be large and imposing, and is often associated with symbols of authority such as churches and castles. This combination of age, size and power over people gives off a mood of oppression and of the protagonists' inherent weakness within the greater scheme of the world. Examples of such Gothic worlds include Franz Kafka's The Trial, Ray Bradbury's Fahrenheit 451, Ira Levin's The Stepford Wives, Ken Kessey's One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest, and the movies The Godfather (1972), Brazil (1985) and Idiocracy (2006). <br /> <br /> <b>Punk</b> refers not to the music movement of the 1970s but to the attitudes behind it: <i>be yourself and fuck anyone who has a problem with it</i>. It is a theme of being a person and an individual rather than just a number or a cog. You - yes, you - are important and your voice is worth being heard, provided you are willing to speak up and accept the potential consequences that come with not following the crowd. It's not a new phenomenon. Most of the 19th century anti-slavery and women's suffrage activists qualify, along side labor activists, Native American/First Nation leaders fighting against displacement, and leaders of some of the 1848 revolutions in Europe, just to give a few examples. <br /> <br /> Stuff from the past decade or so I'd give as examples of Gothic-Punk include the Harry Potter novels, the Luke Cage series on Netflix, and the movie Frozen. None of them involve subcultures or are grimdark, but all involve the protagonists being haunted by the past events and constrained by present circumstances while still striving against those things in the hope for something better.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T14:38:03-07:00","id":1202023,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202020#post1202020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Gothic</b> (<i>not</i> Goth) refers to the Gothic literary genre of the 18th and 19th centuries and it's modern decedents, as well as it's spin-offs (Southern Gothic, German &quot;Shudder Novels&quot;, Pulp Horror, Hammer Horror films, etc.) and to certain traits of the late medieval architecture that played such a huge part in it. Said architecture tends to be large and imposing, and is often associated with symbols of authority such as churches and castles. This combination of age, size and power over people gives off a mood of oppression and of the protagonists' inherent weakness within the greater scheme of the world. Examples of such Gothic worlds include Franz Kafka's The Trial, Ray Bradbury's Fahrenheit 451, Ira Levin's The Stepford Wives, Ken Kessey's One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest, and the movies The Godfather (1972), Brazil (1985) and Idiocracy (2006).<br /> <br /> <b>Punk</b> refers not to the music movement of the 1970s but to the attitudes behind it: <i>be yourself and fuck anyone who has a problem with it</i>. It is a theme of being a person and an individual rather than just a number or a cog. You - yes, you - are important and your voice is worth being heard, provided you are willing to speak up and accept the potential consequences that come with not following the crowd. It's not a new phenomenon. Most of the 19th century anti-slavery and women's suffrage activists qualify, along side labor activists, Native American/First Nation leaders fighting against displacement, and leaders of some of the 1848 revolutions in Europe, just to give a few examples.<br /> <br /> Stuff from the past decade or so I'd give as examples of Gothic-Punk include the Harry Potter novels, the Luke Cage series on Netflix, and the movie Frozen. None of them involve subcultures or are grimdark, but all involve the protagonists being haunted by the past events and constrained by present circumstances while still striving against those things in the hope for something better.</div> </div> </div> </div>Very elegantly summarized. You have softened my opinion of the phrase &quot;gothic-punk&quot; considerably.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Never not tired","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12001-the-nilbog","name":"The Nilbog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12001_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"207"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T14:55:40-07:00","id":1202027,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202020#post1202020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Gothic</b> (<i>not</i> Goth) refers to the Gothic literary genre of the 18th and 19th centuries and it's modern decedents, as well as it's spin-offs (Southern Gothic, German &quot;Shudder Novels&quot;, Pulp Horror, Hammer Horror films, etc.) and to certain traits of the late medieval architecture that played such a huge part in it. Said architecture tends to be large and imposing, and is often associated with symbols of authority such as churches and castles. This combination of age, size and power over people gives off a mood of oppression and of the protagonists' inherent weakness within the greater scheme of the world. Examples of such Gothic worlds include Franz Kafka's The Trial, Ray Bradbury's Fahrenheit 451, Ira Levin's The Stepford Wives, Ken Kessey's One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest, and the movies The Godfather (1972), Brazil (1985) and Idiocracy (2006).<br /> <br /> <b>Punk</b> refers not to the music movement of the 1970s but to the attitudes behind it: <i>be yourself and fuck anyone who has a problem with it</i>. It is a theme of being a person and an individual rather than just a number or a cog. You - yes, you - are important and your voice is worth being heard, provided you are willing to speak up and accept the potential consequences that come with not following the crowd. It's not a new phenomenon. Most of the 19th century anti-slavery and women's suffrage activists qualify, along side labor activists, Native American/First Nation leaders fighting against displacement, and leaders of some of the 1848 revolutions in Europe, just to give a few examples.<br /> <br /> Stuff from the past decade or so I'd give as examples of Gothic-Punk include the Harry Potter novels, the Luke Cage series on Netflix, and the movie Frozen. None of them involve subcultures or are grimdark, but all involve the protagonists being haunted by the past events and constrained by present circumstances while still striving against those things in the hope for something better.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I think a lot of Urban Fantasy books could qualify as gothic-punk too, because they're containing the same elements. Dresden Files, for example, or Alex Verus, totally. Not surprising, the whole UF genre bsically started with WoD...<br /> <br /> To the Gothic part, I always felt, that, while the feeling of opression and insignificance is the primary driving factor, the awe that architecture could induce and the mystique those old secrets and horrors let you to glimpse are also important. Essentially, the larger-than-life feeling, that there is <i>something</i> <i>more </i>out there. Another important thing, I think, is the barbarism associated with the old things.<br /> <br /> Also, punk is mainly the revolutionary mindset and &quot;gives no fuck&quot; attitude and striving to be yourself in the face of opression, but it's also the showing of the world's ugly underbelly unapologetically.<br /> <br /> And most importantly, the contrast between the two, between the old and new, the rich and poor, the haves and have nots, the lofty ideals and the muck of the everyday streets, the individual and the system, etc.<br /> <br /> So, ultimately, yes, I think one could totally separate the gothic-punk themes and style from the goth/punk aesthetical style the books went for, or even from the personal horror aspect. These are three different things, however, they went hand-in hand and strengthened each other and ultimately gave the full picture of Vampire, that's true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":6363,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","user":"PM\u00e1rk","date":". 04-01-2018, 06:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T15:07:42-07:00","id":1202029,"title":"","content":"Someone - and I wish I could remember who so I could give them proper credit - once told me that their epitome of the Gothic Punk ideal was the bit in H.P. Lovecraft's &quot;Call of Cthulhu&quot; when the guy decides to ram Cthulhu in the face with his boat. In the face of something that would seem to make all human life irrelevant, he essentially says &quot;fuck it&quot; and does something ludicrously defiant. And it actually works. Cthulhu is driven back into R'lyeh and the guy gets away.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T15:13:08-07:00","id":1202032,"title":"","content":"What ultimately happened, I think that the writers back in the day had these themes, these <i>feelings</i> and have seen around them the subcultures whose grasped these feelings at the time, but also the world at large and had the same sources which inspired those and also their own experiences with those sources.<br /> <br /> Then, I think, they went and examined these sources (and the subcultures and social movements too) more closely to see which were the main ideas and emotions and impressions behind them. Then they summarized and extrapolated it beyond the subcultures or the original sources, like literature and architecture and named it gothic-punk. In that, gothic stands for some things and punk for others as we discussed.<br /> <br /> What is funny, that while it was, I think, a good choice for naming the end result, because it also provided a tie-in to the subcultures and ultimately, the youth of those days, it also ended up making a lot of misconception and even turning away some people after those subcultures have fallen out from the mainstream's fickle favor.<br /> <br /> Essentially, when goth and punk wasn't <i>chich</i> any more, a lot of people didn't take the effort to understand that gothic-punk is not goth/punk, even if they are connected, by having the same sources.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T15:15:14-07:00","id":1202035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202029#post1202029\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Someone - and I wish I could remember who so I could give them proper credit - once told me that their epitome of the Gothic Punk ideal was the bit in H.P. Lovecraft's &quot;Call of Cthulhu&quot; when the guy decides to ram Cthulhu in the face with his boat. In the face of something that would seem to make all human life irrelevant, he essentially says &quot;fuck it&quot; and does something ludicrously defiant. And it actually works. Cthulhu is driven back into R'lyeh and the guy gets away.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hahha, good one! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T18:20:04-07:00","id":1202082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201973#post1201973\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hmm, could you please describe, as accuratelly, as you can, that specific kind of angst, as you're perceiving it? Also, that it is in the books, or something you perceived in the fanbase's attitude?<br /> <br /> Just to be clear, I'm not nitpicking, I'm really curious! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Understandable, it's not a thing for a lot of people. I like it and in general, metaplot in rpgs aren't a bad thing for me, but hey.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Please, don't get it as me trying to argue about your taste, or choices, those are totally fine, I just want to understand it. It's just a bit strange to me, for two resons:<br /> <br /> 1. The books discussed at length, that gothic-punk is an attitude in WoD, baked into the themes and premises of the settings, not primarily just an aesthetical choice. Sure, it got tangled up with the goth/punk visual style of the game and it's not surprising that a lot of people are confusing the two, but still, the books made it clear what they mean by gothic-punk, philosophycally. I'm just curious, how or why you got to see gothic-punk as just aesthetics, despite the books' descriptions?<br /> <br /> 2. About the aesthetical side: I remember you said that you had bad experiences with said subcultures, so I understand that you don't care for that. However, how you got to like the game, especially 1e, if you don't like the aesthetics even a bit? I'm asking, because for me, the visuals of VtM wasl always an ever-present and very important part of the full impression the game made, so, I'd automatically assume that if someone likes any iteration of the game, they possibly like the aesthetics of the books, at some level. Or do you use text-only versions?<br /> <br /> <br /> Ultimately, if you don't like the themes of gothic-punk and don't like the aesthetics of the game, what drove you to it, or more accurately, why did you pick it up the first place?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 1. I actually sort of appreciate the attitude you are describing, so the attitude in and of itself is not a problem for me.<br /> <br /> It's the subculture stuff and the immense elitism and wangsting that is so often found in specific elements in the WoD fandom (especially within the LARP segment, and I say this as a LARP'er myself) that has made the very concept of &quot;Personal Horror&quot; toxic to me. To me, the concept of Personal Horror is whining and bemoaning about your lost humanity combined with heavy amounts of pretentiousness and pseudo-intellectualism, a disdain for combat or adventure of any kind, outright hostility to anything that can be considered &quot;cool&quot; or &quot;heroic&quot;, a strict adherence to the metaplot (especially the way it was presented in Revised Edition), and may God help you if your Kindred is a good person or carries a katana.<br /> <br /> To me, Personal Horror is epitomized by the premise that whiny Goth/Punk angst is the &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD and that any other playstyle is &quot;playing the game wrong&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now, this is NOT intended to be an attack on the Goth or Punk subcultures in any way, shape, or form. It is simply an explanation as to why I resent terms such as &quot;Personal Horror&quot; and &quot;Gothic Punk&quot; due to toxic elements within the WoD fandom that I have had to deal with both online and in real life.<br /> <br /> 2. I picked up WoD because I wanted a game of urban fantasy and modern adventure, and I don't like The Dresden Files one bit. I sort of used to like the Goth and Punk aesthetics (not so much the music though) and I was a bit of a hybrid between a casual Goth and an anime otaku when I was in middle school and high school. So, it wasn't a problem for me when I first picked up WoD.<br /> <br /> Only after dealing with toxic individuals within these subcultures and the WoD fandom both online and IRL that I began to despise the aesthetics.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T19:48:30-07:00","id":1202109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202020#post1202020\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Gothic</b> (<i>not</i> Goth) refers to the Gothic literary genre of the 18th and 19th centuries and it's modern decedents, as well as it's spin-offs (Southern Gothic, German &quot;Shudder Novels&quot;, Pulp Horror, Hammer Horror films, etc.) and to certain traits of the late medieval architecture that played such a huge part in it. Said architecture tends to be large and imposing, and is often associated with symbols of authority such as churches and castles. This combination of age, size and power over people gives off a mood of oppression and of the protagonists' inherent weakness within the greater scheme of the world. Examples of such Gothic worlds include Franz Kafka's The Trial, Ray Bradbury's Fahrenheit 451, Ira Levin's The Stepford Wives, Ken Kessey's One Flew Over the Cuckoo's Nest, and the movies The Godfather (1972), Brazil (1985) and Idiocracy (2006).</div> </div> </div> </div>I find the Gothic genre also is concerned, thematically, with the ideas of age and decay, both physical and moral. What inspired Gothic literature was the presence of medieval architecture, as writers tried to pen stories that played into the feelings invoked by those old structures. Something I like to call &quot;Desolate Beauty&quot;. The earliest Gothic stories were period pieces, set during those medieval years. Concerning themselves with the plight of monarchs and noble houses. And how they fell to degeneration, and decay.<br /> <br /> Old school Gothic literature is about once great institutions gone to seed. It's also how that very seeding preludes the fall of greatness and the reclamation of it by nature.<br /> <br /> Gothic horror arose from the idea that even the ordered, &quot;proper&quot; structure of modern society is not immune to powers beyond the understanding of mortals. Ghosts, the undead, monsters, even the weather turn against that which once stood proudly. Where even if you think you understand your world, you couldn't be more wrong. And the Gothic Horrors will come and disabuse you of those notions.<br /> <br /> <br /> These themes are central to Vampire: The Masquerade. Institutions built by ancient figures, working within the structures of &quot;noble&quot; houses. Vampires - who believe themselves ageless and informed - are truly paralyzed by fear of the unknown. The persecution of the Caitiff and the Thin Blood is as much a paranoid reaction against what they could be or what powers they could manifest, as it was against any fear of uprising by the young. The Camarilla is a stagnant ruling elite, and the Sabbat are religious extremists who have lost sight of their original ethos. The Independents - the Giovanni, Assamites, Followers of Set, and even Ravnos - dabble in strange powers and even stranger beliefs. All quake in terror of the savages of the wilderness (Werewolves), shrink away from the mortals who wield strange magic(k) new and old (Mages), and become unnerved when the dead (many made by the vampires themselves) fail to stay quiet (Wraiths).<br /> <br /> It doesn't really matter if the vampires wear all black or not. What matters are the themes at play, when it comes to the Gothic.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-02T07:41:06-07:00","id":1202217,"title":"","content":"I think one of the better primers on the subject I can remember reading was <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products/SJG30-6538\" target=\"_blank\">GURPS Screampunk</a>, a small booklet put out as a companion piece for their very popular (and very excellent) <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products/gurps-classic-steampunk\" target=\"_blank\">GURPS Steampunk</a> setting book. It gave a run down of a number of genre tropes and some of the reasons why they existed. The one I remember most was the idea that the sins of the father (or mother) will often be visited upon the son (or daughter), regardless of any sense of justice or morality. That idea ended up strongly coloring my view of the Metis in Werewolf, and I think it applies pretty well to Vampire as well, especially when it comes to Sire/Childe relations. <br /> <br /> And yes, the Kindred are a very cowardly and superstitious lot. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-02T17:14:39-07:00","id":1202356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202109#post1202109\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find the Gothic genre also is concerned, thematically, with the ideas of age and decay, both physical and moral. What inspired Gothic literature was the presence of medieval architecture, as writers tried to pen stories that played into the feelings invoked by those old structures. Something I like to call &quot;Desolate Beauty&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>And I believe the modern manifestation of that is the notion of &quot;urban decay&quot;, which was always very prominent in WoD and it's aesthetical representation. At least to me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-02T18:16:41-07:00","id":1202384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202217#post1202217\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think one of the better primers on the subject I can remember reading was <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products/SJG30-6538\" target=\"_blank\">GURPS Screampunk</a>, a small booklet put out as a companion piece for their very popular (and very excellent) <a href=\"http://www.warehouse23.com/products/gurps-classic-steampunk\" target=\"_blank\">GURPS Steampunk</a> setting book. It gave a run down of a number of genre tropes and some of the reasons why they existed. The one I remember most was the idea that the sins of the father (or mother) will often be visited upon the son (or daughter), regardless of any sense of justice or morality. That idea ended up strongly coloring my view of the Metis in Werewolf, and I think it applies pretty well to Vampire as well, especially when it comes to Sire/Childe relations.<br /> <br /> And yes, the Kindred are a very cowardly and superstitious lot. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>The &quot;Sins of the Father&quot; idea can also manifest in an interesting manner with Mages.<br /> <br /> There is, of course, the relationship between a mage and her Mentor. Mentors shape the worldviews of the Apprentices, as well as their methods. If there is a flaw in the mentor's methodology, that flaw may be passed onto their student. Books, items, and properties are also often left to apprentices, which can carry their own curses. Finally, the reputation or practices of the Mentor can mold or reflect on the student. Flaws such as Twisted Apprenticeship and Diabolical Mentor have heavy impacts on mages who have them, whether from a social context, or a more metaphysical one.<br /> <br /> On an even more abstract level, Sins of the Father could come into play between a mage and her previous incarnations. A fascinating avenue of storytelling, as the mage both IS and IS NOT her prior self. Creating interesting ideas to explore, since &quot;her&quot; own actions come back to haunt her. Works quite well with a Flaw like Throwback.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-07T14:57:59-07:00","id":1204307,"title":"","content":"The game and the books should be used as a toolkit to tell the kind of stories you want in a world full of vampires, werewolfs, magicians, ghosts, and faeries. A Dresden Files approach is just as good as any of the versions of the Classic World of Darkness as is anywhere inbetween.<br /> <br /> Now I prefer the game to be in its roots in the horror genre (classic Universal/Hammer and Anne Rice inspirations) rather than urban fantasy, but honestly most people have not played it as a game of personal horror.<br /> <br /> But certainly, even as originally written the kind of horror found in Vampire and Wraith was very different from the presentation of Werewolf, Mage, and Changeling. So there has always been a variety of interpretations of how dark things are.<br /> <br /> I would say an urban fantasy style chronicle is best suited for low powered mortals using books like Sorceror, Mediums, Arcanum, and Inquisition rather than one of the major game lines and playing vampires or werewolves. I just don't understand why someone would want to play Vampire, but not really want to roleplay vampires.<br /> <br /> In terms of my personal play style, I prefer a &quot;Gothic Noir&quot; approach rather than Gothic Punk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-07T15:19:35-07:00","id":1204315,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202082#post1202082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. I actually sort of appreciate the attitude you are describing, so the attitude in and of itself is not a problem for me.<br /> <br /> It's the subculture stuff and the immense elitism and wangsting that is so often found in specific elements in the WoD fandom (especially within the LARP segment, and I say this as a LARP'er myself) that has made the very concept of &quot;Personal Horror&quot; toxic to me. To me, the concept of Personal Horror is whining and bemoaning about your lost humanity combined with heavy amounts of pretentiousness and pseudo-intellectualism, a disdain for combat or adventure of any kind, outright hostility to anything that can be considered &quot;cool&quot; or &quot;heroic&quot;, a strict adherence to the metaplot (especially the way it was presented in Revised Edition), and may God help you if your Kindred is a good person or carries a katana.<br /> <br /> To me, Personal Horror is epitomized by the premise that whiny Goth/Punk angst is the &quot;One True Way&quot; to play WoD and that any other playstyle is &quot;playing the game wrong&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now, this is NOT intended to be an attack on the Goth or Punk subcultures in any way, shape, or form. It is simply an explanation as to why I resent terms such as &quot;Personal Horror&quot; and &quot;Gothic Punk&quot; due to toxic elements within the WoD fandom that I have had to deal with both online and in real life.</div> </div> </div> </div>There have been two great reactionary movements within the fan base as the game came out and play developed.<br /> <br /> The first was in reaction to people who clearly were not playing their game's characters or basic premise. Instead of being vampires, they were simply strange people with dietary restrictions (easily handwaved by various means) who used superpowers. This was mocked as Fishmalks, Vampions, and varying degrees of camp. These kind of games did not seem to exist in the same world as the reactionaries imagined the game should be.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the result was too many players seemed to confuse &quot;horror&quot; with &quot;whining&quot; and life avoidance or &quot;everything is grimdark/dying and sucks anyway.&quot; The result was exceedingly depressing games that weren't fun. This more or less defines the Revised era although not completely. This is mocked in its own way as well.<br /> <br /> The second was in reaction to that as horror and depression are not the same thing, but by that time the classic World of Darkness was dead as a game line. These people carried on and worked on their own chronicles to achieve the balance they wanted.<br /> <br /> I think there can still be a wide range of playing styles while still keeping the game in line with the horror genre, even within Vampire itself. There is a place for Hannibal King style PCs. There is even a place for an entire coterie of vampire PCs whose members are trying to hold onto their humanity while existing in the cannibalistic society of the Kindred. It's just that the NPCs have to be played in a way consistent with the setting while allowing the PCs to explore their own thing. And that is fine. The Sabbat's monstrous Paths of Enlightenment exists in the same world as Golconda. Vampires can be Louis as well as Lestat. They can be Hannibal King as well as Dracula. And PCs can confront the horror of the Beast and that they can only survive by feeding off the blood of the living in a way that is moral and also not whining. But it also means that not all kinds of plots are best suited for certain style of play.<br /> <br /> <br /> And toxic players are never fun regardless of the game or style of play. They just suck.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-07T19:38:14-07:00","id":1204392,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1204307#post1204307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> but honestly most people have not played it as a game of personal horror.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because, honestly, personal horror, while could be very rewarding, is <i>very</i> tiring on the long run and I'd even say, boring as hell. Really, as I said earlier, how long could you mourn the dreadfull loss of humanity, because it becomes silly?<br /> <br /> I believe personal horror is most useful, when it's manifesting in small, everyday things, but in a disturbing way (and you should be careful even with that, no one wants to hear the hundredth time how inhuman it is to drink blood), or, with very powerfull scenes, but in this latter case, not that frequently.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But certainly, even as originally written the kind of horror found in Vampire and Wraith was very different from the presentation of Werewolf, Mage, and Changeling. So there has always been a variety of interpretations of how dark things are. </div> </div> </div>Yup.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would say an urban fantasy style chronicle is best suited for low powered mortals using books like Sorceror, Mediums, Arcanum, and Inquisition rather than one of the major game lines and playing vampires or werewolves. I just don't understand why someone would want to play Vampire, but not really want to roleplay vampires. </div> </div> </div>Same reason people bought the countless urban fantasy novels and watched tv-series and animes with vampires who are totally like humans in every instance, sans occasionally drinking blood, superpowers and <i>maybe</i> sun allergy. As you said, they want &quot;simply strange people with dietary restrictions (easily handwaved by various means) who used superpowers&quot;. That simple.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In terms of my personal play style, I prefer a &quot;Gothic Noir&quot; approach rather than Gothic Punk. </div> </div> </div>Could you describe that? I have some ideas, but it'd be good to read your own definition! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Also, could you explain, why you eschewed the punk part?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Unfortunately, the result was too many players seemed to confuse &quot;horror&quot; with &quot;whining&quot; and life avoidance or &quot;everything is grimdark/dying and sucks anyway.&quot; The result was exceedingly depressing games that weren't fun. This more or less defines the Revised era although not completely. This is mocked in its own way as well. </div> </div> </div>I'd add another factor: Vampire attracted a lot of people, who were really into the approach the writers have pressed frequently, meaning, putting emphasis on the character's inner life, struggles with personal problems and demons, emotional relationships and such. Those people also frequently sneered at the things they perceived as &quot;D&amp;D with vampires&quot;, like combat, &quot;adventure&quot;, epic plots, etc. and also at the people not completely into the artsy, navel-gazing, philosophyzing playing style, centered mainly around social interaction. LARP is <i>especially </i>frequented by those players, who essentially see the game as an amateur drama class and/or place to act out their real personal dramas with other people.<br /> <br /> Of course the two styles, the vampions and the Jane Austen-novel vampires, are extremes. Frankly, I think the silent majority always played somewhere in-between and also, the books had material for both.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-08T14:22:31-07:00","id":1204691,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1204315#post1204315\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There have been two great reactionary movements within the fan base as the game came out and play developed.<br /> <br /> The first was in reaction to people who clearly were not playing their game's characters or basic premise. Instead of being vampires, they were simply strange people with dietary restrictions (easily handwaved by various means) who used superpowers. This was mocked as Fishmalks, Vampions, and varying degrees of camp. These kind of games did not seem to exist in the same world as the reactionaries imagined the game should be.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fishmalks were something different. Vampions were one thing, but the Fishmalk was just there to do random comedy gags and not much else, which makes it difficult to get along with any game that's grounded on even slightly more serious assumptions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T14:24:11-07:00","id":1204977,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1202082#post1202082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's the subculture stuff and the immense elitism and wangsting that is so often found in specific elements in the WoD fandom (especially within the LARP segment, and I say this as a LARP'er myself) that has made the very concept of &quot;Personal Horror&quot; toxic to me. To me, the concept of Personal Horror is whining and bemoaning about your lost humanity combined with heavy amounts of pretentiousness and pseudo-intellectualism, a disdain for combat or adventure of any kind, outright hostility to anything that can be considered &quot;cool&quot; or &quot;heroic&quot;, a strict adherence to the metaplot (especially the way it was presented in Revised Edition), and may God help you if your Kindred is a good person or carries a katana.</div> </div> </div> </div>I &quot;liked&quot; your post because you've introduced me to the word &quot;wangst,&quot; which is a great weapon that I plan to wield against some of the bad anime characters I've encountered. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I'd like to suggest that you distinguish between &quot;goth&quot; and &quot;emo&quot; in addition to distinguishing between &quot;gothic&quot; and &quot;goth,&quot; though it's probably not all that important. It reminds me of a certain South Park episode.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13713,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","user":"Wilson","date":". 04-09-2018, 05:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T16:20:53-07:00","id":1205004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wilson</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1204977#post1204977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I &quot;liked&quot; your post because you've introduced me to the word &quot;wangst,&quot; which is a great weapon that I plan to wield against some of the bad anime characters I've encountered. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I'd like to suggest that you distinguish between &quot;goth&quot; and &quot;emo&quot; in addition to distinguishing between &quot;gothic&quot; and &quot;goth,&quot; though it's probably not all that important. It reminds me of a certain South Park episode.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> To be fair, Goth and Emo, while different in terms of music and fashion choices, are eerily similar in their nihilistic ideologies. At least from what I have had to deal with from playing with Goths in real life, anyway.<br /> <br /> I don't mind Gothic all that much, if we're talking about Gothic fiction and the like. Edgar Allan Poe is one of my favorite poets and writers, after all.<br /> <br /> I'm all in favor of Edgar Allan Poe and Lord Byron, but leave The Sisters of Mercy and Type O Negative at the door at my games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T16:54:03-07:00","id":1205013,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1205004#post1205004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> To be fair, Goth and Emo, while different in terms of music and fashion choices, are eerily similar in their nihilistic ideologies. At least from what I have had to deal with from playing with Goths in real life, anyway.<br /> <br /> I don't mind Gothic all that much, if we're talking about Gothic fiction and the like. Edgar Allan Poe is one of my favorite poets and writers, after all.<br /> <br /> I'm all in favor of Edgar Allan Poe and Lord Byron, but leave The Sisters of Mercy and Type O Negative at the door at my games.</div> </div> </div> </div>That reminded me that while I really loved the mood and aesthetics of the books, musically, funnily, I wasn't really into goth music, back in the day. In the last several years, I developed a certain taste for some of it, but overall I was and am mostly of a symphonic metal and gothic metal guy. For goth music, I jave to be in a certain mindset and mood to enjoy.<br /> <br /> Honestly, who the f*ck could work out while listening to Sisters????? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T19:29:43-07:00","id":1205059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1205013#post1205013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That reminded me that while I really loved the mood and aesthetics of the books, musically, funnily, I wasn't really into goth music, back in the day. In the last several years, I developed a certain taste for some of it, but overall I was and am mostly of a symphonic metal and gothic metal guy. For goth music, I jave to be in a certain mindset and mood to enjoy.<br /> <br /> Honestly, who the f*ck could work out while listening to Sisters????? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I was always more of a classic rock and oldies kind of guy. I love the hits of the 50's, 60's, and 70's!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1579-camilla","name":"Camilla","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1579_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-19T10:07:01-07:00","id":1208932,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PM\u00e1rk</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1205013#post1205013\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was and am mostly of a symphonic metal and gothic metal guy.</div> </div> </div> </div>I just looked up &quot;symphonic metal&quot; on Youtube. Huge thumbs up! Thank you! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13713-wilson","name":"Wilson","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"182"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-19T17:28:01-07:00","id":1209066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wilson</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1208932#post1208932\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I just looked up &quot;symphonic metal&quot; on Youtube. Huge thumbs up! Thank you! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Hahha, welcome to the fold! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I just like bombastic, theatrical sounds and female vocals quite a lot, I'm glad you're anjoying it too! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Admittedly, after Nightwish's success back in the day, the Scandinavian symph-metal bands became dime a dozen, but the large names more-or-less stood the test of time. <br /> <br /> I can also recommend Mandragora Scream (they are more into the cabaret-style metal), Theatres des Vampires (cheesy as hell, but the later albums are quite enjoyable, IMO and immediately put me into the mindspace I was in when I first read the Vampire corebook).<br /> <br /> 69 Eyes and Powerwolf are on a whole different level of cheesyness, but in a good, &quot;not-taking-ourselves-seriously-at-all&quot; way, in my book and they are good for letting loose and pumping up blood pressure.<br /> <br /> On a different note, I can't recommend enough the OST of the Only Lovers Left Alive movie. My go-to VtM ambient music album.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-19T21:31:43-07:00","id":1209115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>The Nilbog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness#post1195123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Am I stupid for thinking that it's possible to play the World of Darkness as less, well... dark?<br /> <br /> Maybe it's just that I'm an ultra-idealistic millennial manchild, but I've always seen the WoD as a great urban fantasy setting that can run the whole spectrum of tones and moods without sacrificing its famous thematic complexity. I enjoy it as a world with dark shadows, yes, but brilliant lights and amazing things as well. I just don't see the fun in making the answer to everything &quot;It's the World of Darkness, so everything's terrible.&quot;<br /> <br /> Am I alone in this? Am I profoundly missing the point, or does my interpretation hold some merit?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> I don't remember which one it was, but I came across a live play series on youtube where they retconned the universe so that there was no masquerade. Apparently some mages went back in time and changed something (I don't remember the specifics), but one of the side effects was that vampires were no longer harmed by sunlight, thus allowing them to freely operate during the day. Another side effect was that most (if not all) of the supernatural creatures had openly announced themselves to the public and were living as citizens among human society.<br /> <br /> <br /> Of course, since everybody knew about their existence, the governments of the world needed a way to maintain order, so they created new branches of law enforcement that were specifically trained to deal with criminal elements of the supernatural persuasion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-20T13:37:46-07:00","id":1209379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1209115#post1209115\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> I don't remember which one it was, but I came across a live play series on youtube where they retconned the universe so that there was no masquerade. Apparently some mages went back in time and changed something (I don't remember the specifics), but one of the side effects was that vampires were no longer harmed by sunlight, thus allowing them to freely operate during the day. Another side effect was that most (if not all) of the supernatural creatures had openly announced themselves to the public and were living as citizens among human society.<br /> <br /> <br /> Of course, since everybody knew about their existence, the governments of the world needed a way to maintain order, so they created new branches of law enforcement that were specifically trained to deal with criminal elements of the supernatural persuasion.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heh, somebody did read Anita Blake. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T10:57:38-07:00","id":1244205,"title":"","content":"When coming up with story ideas set in the World of Darkness (mainly Werewolf: The Apocalypse), I've always preferred a more lighter tone. Yes, Pentex is still scheming about in the background, random people still transform into formori but I never portray them as overwhelmingly powerful and unstoppable as the fluff states. I know most people dislike how over-the-top evil Pentex can be but, personally, I love it as I think it makes for a more fun source of villainy. <br /> <br /> Not to mention, a lot of my Bone Gnawer character's friends and allies tend to be Orphans, Nosferatu, Changelings, Ratkin, etc., as the disdain, paranoia and outright hatred between groups is toned WAY down, reserved for a few fanatical members. Not that everyone is holding hands and singing around the campfire, but they're more likely to go to the local bar and hash things out over a few beers than try to kill each other at first sight.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1195542#post1195542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've found it's fairly easy to run stuff in the vein of, say, the first season or two of Angel or The Dresden Files books. Usually using caitiff, sorcerers (sometimes orphans), mediums, old style mummies, psychics, changelings and sometimes risen or tribeless werewolves or fera. .</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll be honest, I'd play the hell out of that version of the World of Darkness. A bit lower on the power scale but probably a lot easier to play crossover games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24162-samuraijake07","name":"samuraijake07","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24162_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-08-09T14:00:11-07:00","id":1244268,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>samuraijake07</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1244205#post1244205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When coming up with story ideas set in the World of Darkness (mainly Werewolf: The Apocalypse), I've always preferred a more lighter tone. Yes, Pentex is still scheming about in the background, random people still transform into formori but I never portray them as overwhelmingly powerful and unstoppable as the fluff states. I know most people dislike how over-the-top evil Pentex can be but, personally, I love it as I think it makes for a more fun source of villainy.<br /> <br /> Not to mention, a lot of my Bone Gnawer character's friends and allies tend to be Orphans, Nosferatu, Changelings, Ratkin, etc., as the disdain, paranoia and outright hatred between groups is toned WAY down, reserved for a few fanatical members. Not that everyone is holding hands and singing around the campfire, but they're more likely to go to the local bar and hash things out over a few beers than try to kill each other at first sight.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'll be honest, I'd play the hell out of that version of the World of Darkness. A bit lower on the power scale but probably a lot easier to play crossover games.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, I frequently have an itch to play a more &quot;generic&quot; urban fantasy, with lighter tones, less extreme antagonism between groups, less cosmic-scale conflicts and with a more generic take on the supernaturals.<br /> <br /> Something, like the Mercy Thompson books series, for example.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If nothing worked, then let's think!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6363-pm\u00e1rk","name":"PM\u00e1rk","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6363_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2363"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-24T08:29:48-07:00","id":1493633,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Camilla</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1195123-world-of-darkness-hold-the-darkness?p=1201538#post1201538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Dude, I've been running my Vampire games as a &quot;less dark&quot; World of Darkness for years.<br /> <br /> No Metaplot, No Personal Horror, No Gothic-Punk stuff (my games are more anime-influenced), Vampires can be heroic good guys if they choose to do so, more Trenchcoats and Katanas than you can shake a stick at, and the 90's fan-game Senshi: The Merchandising is declared canon in my personal games, while Kindred of the East and Demon: The Fallen are declared non-canon.<br /> <br /> I personally found the idea of Personal Horror to be stuffy and boring, but other people like it and there's nothing wrong with that either. You do you, and I'll do me and so on.<br /> <br /> Long story short, it's YOUR game. Make it what you want it to be. As long as everyone is having fun, there is NO &quot;wrong way&quot; to play World of Darkness or any other role-playing game for that matter.</div> </div> </div> </div>Gonna necro this thread (sorry, I have to every now and then <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> ) to say that I recently had a request to make a sheet for Senshi: The Merchandising, and if anyone is interested, here's a link: <a href=\"https://mrgone.rocksolidshells.com/pdf/MiscOWOD/SenshiTheMerchandising_2-Page_Interactive.pdf\" target=\"_blank\">https://mrgone.rocksolidshells.com/p...nteractive.pdf</a><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://mrgone.rocksolidshells.com/\" target=\"_blank\">MrGone's Character Sheets</a><br /> For all your WoD Character Sheet Needs<br /> _________________________________<br /> Buy books &amp; pdfs from DTRPG and support my web site! <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=55414\" target=\"_blank\">Click Here</a><br /> Support my site on Patreon! <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/mrgone\" target=\"_blank\">Click Here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24-mr-gone","name":"Mr Gone","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2695"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-24T09:34:57-07:00","id":1493634,"title":"","content":"Look, if you are going to do it right playing anything in the World of Darkness should involve so much despair, misery and darkness it is like your very soul is force fed ashes while wearing only charcoal dust and staring at the abyss until the Abyss gets uncomfortable and goes to complain to HR.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-09-24T11:48:56-07:00","id":1493641,"title":"","content":"Requiem and CoD in general fit the urban fantasy mold much better than masquerade, especially the 2nd editions who toned down A LOT of stuff, a bit too much in my opinion but still.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27142-newb95","name":"Newb95","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"502"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}